prt‐get(8)										 prt‐get(8)

NAME
     prt-get  -	 an  advanced  package	management  tool  to  be  used with pkgutils from CRUX (see
     http://www.crux.nu)

SYNOPSIS
     prt-get command [options] <arguments>

DESCRIPTION
     prt-get is a package management tool which provides additional functionality to crux’  package
     management	 system.  It works with the local ports tree and is therefore fully compatible with
     ports(8) and pkgmk(8)/pkgadd(8). It offers the following features:

	•   abstract ports installation/update from file system

	•   install/update a list of packages with one command

	•   list dependencies for a list of packages

	•   show information about ports

	•   search within the ports

     What prt-get basically does is installing and upgrading packages, using pkgmk and pkgadd.	Ad‐
     ditionally, you don’t have be in the port’s directory to call prt-get. prt-get will search for
     the  respective  port itself in a list of directories specified in /etc/prt-get.conf. This al‐
     lows you to just install or update a package, without caring where it actually is	located	 on
     your file system.

     prt-get  also  offers  some  features  like searching ports by name, showing information about
     ports (without installing them of course) and can list the dependencies listed in the Pkgfile,
     and provide a complete dependency list for a port. Note that dependencies are  no	requirement
     for  crux	packages and therefore not always accurate. There’s a possiblity to use an external
     dependency map to get dependency listings for ports which don’t provide them in their Pkgfile.

     prt-get has a test mode so you can see what effect an install/update operation would have. Use
     the --test switch for this (see also: OPTIONS)

RETURN VALUE
     prt-get returns 0 on success and a non‐zero value otherwise (exact value ‐> meaning mapping to
     be determined...)

COMMANDS
     prt-get uses so called commands, which always have to be the first non‐option argument passed.
     This is very similar to cvs(1).  [command] can be one of the following:

     install [--margs] [--aargs] [--log] <package1> [<package2> ...]
	    install all packages in the listed order. Note that you can do this from any directory

     depinst [--margs] [--aargs] [--log] <package1> [<package2> ...]
	    install all packages in the listed order including their dependencies.  Note that  out‐
	    dated packages won’t be updated.

     grpinst [--margs] [--aargs] [--log] <package1> [<package2> ...]
	    install  all  packages  in	the  listed  order, but stop if installation of one package
	    fails. This can be used to install packages where package2 depends on package1

     update [--margs] [--aargs] [--log] <package1> [<package2> ...]
	    update all packages listed in this order

     remove <package1> [<package2> ...]
	    remove packages listed in this order

     sysup [--nodeps]
	    Update all installed packages which are outdated. Sorts by dependencies by default.	 If
	    you	 don’t	want prt‐get to resolve the dependencies, use the ‐‐nodeps switch. Also see
	    the lock and unlock commands to keep the currently installed versions,  even  if  there
	    are	 changes  in the ports tree. If you want to update only packages which have a lower
	    version installed than the one in the ports tree, use the ‐‐prefer‐higher option.

     lock <package1> [<package2>...]
	    Do not update these packages in a sysup operation

     sync [<repo> ...]
	    (Alternative to ports ‐u) Synchronize the  local  ports  collections  with	the  remote
	    servers.  If  any arguments are passed, the operation is restricted to those ports col‐
	    lections; otherwise all active collections will be brought up to  date.   A	 collection
	    definition	in /etc/ports is considered active if its suffix matches one of the drivers
	    in /etc/ports/drivers. Hence it is possible to disable a  collection  by  changing	the
	    suffix,  or to disable an entire class of ports collections by renaming the correspond‐
	    ing driver.

     unlock <package1> [<package2>...]
	    Remove lock from these packages

     listlocked [‐v|‐vv]
	    List names of packages which are locked. As always, ‐v will additionally show the  ver‐
	    sion,  and ‐vv also includes the description from the Pkgfile. Note that the ‐vv switch
	    will slow down the operation remarkably.

     diff [‐‐all] [<package1> <package2> ...]
	    show differences between installed packages and ports in the ports tree.  If  arguments
	    are	 given,	 shows	only  differences for these packages, otherwise all differences are
	    shown. It’s also possible to use shell like wildcards for the diff command.	 Make  sure
	    you	 escape	 where	needed.	 Locked	 packages  are	only displayed if you use the ‐‐all
	    switch. If you want to see only diffs which have a lower version installed than the one
	    in the ports tree, use the ‐‐prefer‐higher option.

     quickdiff
	    prints a simple list of packages which have a different version in the ports tree  than
	    what  is installed. This is meant as an input for prt-get update, so you can update all
	    ports on your system with one simple command. If you want to see only diffs which  have
	    a  lower  version installed than the one in the ports tree, use the ‐‐prefer‐higher op‐
	    tion.

     search [-v|-vv] [‐‐path] [‐‐regex]<expr>
	    Search the ports tree for expr in their name

     dsearch [-v|-vv] [‐‐path] [‐‐regex]<expr>
	    Search the ports tree for expr in both name and description. The search in the descrip‐
	    tion is not case sensitive. Note that this requires	 prt-get  to  read  every  Pkgfile,
	    which  makes  it rather slow; if you like this, consider using the cache functionality,
	    so you only have to spend this time once after updating the ports  tree  has  been	up‐
	    dated.

     fsearch [‐‐full] [‐‐regex] <pattern>
	    Search  the ports tree for pattern as file name in their footprint. When called without
	    ’‐‐full’, strips the directories from the file names before	 matching;  this  behaviour
	    will  change  in  prt‐get 0.6, where full path search will be default. Pattern can be a
	    shell‐like wildcard pattern (e.g prt‐get fsearch "*.h") or regexps.

     info <port>
	    Print available info for a port

     path <port>
	    Print the path of a port

     readme <port>
	    Print the port’s README file if it exists; if set, uses $PAGER

     depends <package1> [<package2> ...]
	    print a recursive list of dependencies to install the packages passed as  argument.	 It
	    shows a list with ports which are found, plus a list of ports which are missing

     quickdep <package1> [<package2> ...]
	    print a simple list of recursive dependencies for all the ports passed as argument in a
	    simple  format  to be used by prt-get install to make an install including dependencies
	    Does not display dependencies which are not in the ports tree

     dependent <package>
	    print a list of package which depend on package.

	    Usually shows dependent packages which are installed. To see all dependencies, add	the
	    ‐‐all switch; use ‐‐recursive to get a recursive list (without duplication), and ‐‐tree
	    to get a nicely indented one

     deptree <package>
	    print  a  tree  of the dependencies of the package package.	 Subtrees already shown are
	    marked with ’‐‐>’ to save some space, in order to show them all, add the ‐‐all switch

     dup [‐v] [format]
	    List ports which can be found in multiple directories configured  in  /etc/prt‐get.conf
	    Use	 the  verbose switch to simulate the output of version 5.12 and older (likely to go
	    away in the future). The format string can be used to create  user	specified  formats.
	    The following symbols are currently replaced:

	•   %n -> name of the port

	•   %p1 -> Full path (including name) to port taking precendence

	•   %p2 -> Full path (including name) to port being hidden

	•   %v1 -> Version of port taking precendence

	•   %v2 -> Version of port being hidden

     list [-v|-vv] [filter] [‐‐path] [‐‐regex]
	    List  ports	 available in the ports tree. It’s basically the same as ports -l but looks
	    in all directories specified in /etc/prt-get.conf.	It’s also  possible  to	 use  shell
	    like wildcards for the list command. Make sure you escape where needed

     printf <format string1> [--sort=<format string2>] [--filter=<filter>]
	    Print  formated  port list format string can contain variables, which are replaced like
	    this:

	•   %n -> name

	•   %p -> path

	•   %v -> version

	•   %r -> release

	•   %d -> description

	•   %e -> dependencies

	•   %u -> URL

	•   %P ‐> Packager

	•   %M ‐> Maintainer

	•   %R ‐> Readme ("yes"/"no")

	•   %E ‐> pre‐install script ("yes"/"no")

	•   %O ‐> post‐install script ("yes"/"no")

	•   %l ‐> is locked ("yes"/"no")

	•   %i -> "no" if not installed, "yes" if it’s installed and up to date and "diff" if  it’s
	    installed and a new version is in the ports tree.

	    Use	 "\n" and "\t" to format your output (no additional format specified suported). The
	    optional format string2 can contain the same variables as format string1 and is used to
	    sort the output. You can specify a wildcard filter to filter by package name.

     listinst [-v|-vv] [filter] [‐‐regex] [‐‐depsort]
	    List installed ports. It’s basically the same as pkginfo -i,  but  omits  version  when
	    called without verbose (-v, -vv) switch. Plus it is notably faster in my tests. -v adds
	    version information, -vv adds version and description.  Warning: -vv will slow down the
	    process because it requires prt-get to scan both the ports database and the ports tree.
	    It’s  also possible to use shell like wildcards for the listinst command. Make sure you
	    escape where needed. Finally, by default it’s sorted alphabetically; use the  --depsort
	    switch to sort by dependencies

     listorphans [-v|-vv]
	    List installed ports which have no dependent packages

     isinst <package>
	    Check whether a package is installed. The same as pkginfo -i|grep ˆpackage

     current <package>
	    Prints out the version of the currently installed package

     ls [‐‐path] <package>
	    Prints out a listing of the port’s directory

     cat <package> [<file>]
	    Prints  out	 the file to stdout. If <file> is not specified, ’Pkgfile’ is used. If set,
	    uses $PAGER

     edit <package> <file>
	    Edit the file using the editor specified  in  the  $EDITOR	environment  variable.	 If
	    <file> is not specified, ’Pkgfile’ is used

     help   Shows a help screen

     dumpconfig
	    Dump the configuration to the current terminal

     version
	    Shows the current version of prt-get

     cache  create a cache file from the ports tree to be used by prt-get using the --cache option.
	    Remember to run prt-get cache each time you update the ports tree

OPTIONS
     ‐f, ‐i
	    Force install; Implies ’pkgadd ‐f’; same as ‐‐aargs=‐f

     ‐fr    Force rebuild, Implies ’pkgmk ‐f’; same as ‐‐margs=‐f

     ‐um    Update md5sum, implies ’pkgmk ‐um’; same as ‐‐margs=‐um

     ‐im    Ignore md5sum, implies ’pkgmk ‐im’; same as ‐‐margs=‐im

     ‐us    Update signature, implies ’pkgmk ‐us’; same as ‐‐margs=‐us

     ‐is    Ignore signature, implies ’pkgmk ‐is’; same as ‐‐margs=‐is

     ‐uf    Update footprint, implies ’pkgmk ‐uf’; same as ‐‐margs=‐uf

     ‐if    Ignore footprint, implies ’pkgmk ‐if’; same as ‐‐margs=‐if

     ‐ns    No stripping, implies ’pkgmk ‐ns’; same as ‐‐margs=‐ns

     ‐kw    Keep working directory, implies ’pkgmk ‐kw’; same as ‐‐margs=‐kw

     ‐‐ignore=<package1,package2,...>
	    Don’t install those packages, even if they’re listed as dependency

     --cache
	    Use cache file for this command

     --test
	    Dry	 run,  don’t  actually	install	 anything,  mostly interesting for prt-get install,
	    prt-get grpinst, prt-get update, prt-get sysup

     --pre‐install
	    Execute pre‐install script if it’s there

     --post‐install
	    Execute post‐install script if it’s there

     --install‐scripts
	    Execute pre‐install and post‐install script if they’re there

     --no‐std‐config
	    Don’t parse the default configuration file

     --config‐prepend=<string>
	    Prepend <string> to configuration

     --config‐append=<string>
	    Append <string> to configuration

     --config‐set=<string>
	    Set <string> in configuration, overriding configuration file

     -v, -vv
	    verbose and more verbose, relevant for prt-get search, prt-get list verbose shows  ver‐
	    sion of a port, more verbose shows version and description (if available)

     --path
	    Show path info; used in ’search’, ’dsearch’, ’list’ and ’depends’

     --regex
	    Interpret filter and search pattern as regular expression

     --margs="...", e.g. --margs="-im"
	    arguments to be passed to pkgmk, relevant for prt-get install, prt-get grpinst, prt-get
	    update note that -d is already passed to pkgmk anyway

     --aargs="...", e.g. --aargs="-f"
	    arguments  to  be  passed  to  pkgadd,  relevant  for prt-get install, prt-get grpinst,
	    prt-get update

     --rargs="..."
	    arguments to be passed to pkgrm; currently not used, implemented for  future  uses	and
	    consistency reasons

     --prefer‐higher, ‐ph
	    Make  prt‐get  parse the version strings and prefer the higher version, even if the one
	    found in the ports tree is lower. Will influence diff, quickdiff and sysup.

     --strict‐diff, ‐sd
	    Override the ’preferhigher’ configuration option

     --config=<file>
	    Use alternative configuration file to read ports directories from

     --install‐root=<dir>
	    Use <dir> as root directory for your installation; allows to install to a different di‐
	    rectory than ’/’. In daily usage, this option is not required; helpful if you’re devel‐
	    oping an independent installation.

     --log  Write build output to log file

CONFIGURATION
     See man prt-get.conf(5)

EXAMPLES
     prt-get install irssi
	    download, build and install irssi, with one simple command

     prt-get install indent mplayer
	    install indent and mplayer

     prt-get update openssh
	    update your current version of openssh :-)

     prt-get info kdelibs
	    show info about kdelibs

     prt-get search icq
	    Search all ports containing ’icq’ in their name

     prt-get grpinst --test ‘prt-get quickdep kdenetwork‘
	    Show what would happen if you installed all packages needed for kdenetwork (test mode).
	    Remove --test to install kdenetwork and all it’s dependencies.  Remember  that  grpinst
	    stops installing when one installing of one package fails

     prt-get update ‘prt-get quickdiff‘
	    Update all packages where a different version is in the ports tree

